  "narrative": "The 1970s was a transformative time for Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture. During this era, natural textures were celebrated as part of the growing black pride movement. The afro, created by combing out natural growth or using chemical curling products, became a powerful symbol of identity and self-expression. Cornrows, traditional in many African cultures, were also gaining popularity as a way to adorn the hair without causing breakage. Box braids, another protective style, allowed women to add thickness and versatility to their hairstyles while minimizing manipulation. While documentation from this era is limited, it's clear that Black and African women were embracing their natural textures and exploring new ways to express themselves through their hair.",
